WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.347
The serving options, and what each is for.

2
00:00:02.627 --> 00:00:09.667
The landscape changes quickly, so here is the state at this lesson’s review date, from each project’s own documentation.

3
00:00:09.947 --> 00:00:14.747
vLLM is the general-purpose GPU server most teams reach for.

4
00:00:15.027 --> 00:00:32.541
Its docs list continuous batching, PagedAttention, prefix caching, many quantization formats (FP8, INT8, INT4, GPTQ, AWQ, GGUF, and others), multi-LoRA serving, speculative decoding, and an OpenAI-compatible API server.

5
00:00:32.821 --> 00:00:42.208
SGLang is a comparable high-performance serving framework, which its project describes as optimized for agentic workloads and large-scale serving.

6
00:00:42.488 --> 00:00:45.816
Choose between them by benchmarking your model on your hardware.

7
00:00:46.096 --> 00:00:46.864
llama.

8
00:00:47.144 --> 00:00:54.973
cpp and its llama-server run GGUF models (usually quantized) on CPUs, Apple silicon, and GPUs.

9
00:00:55.253 --> 00:01:04.085
The server offers OpenAI-compatible routes, continuous batching with parallel slots, and grammar- or JSON-schema-constrained output.

10
00:01:04.365 --> 00:01:09.848
It is the natural choice for laptops, edge devices, and modest single-machine deployments.

11
00:01:10.128 --> 00:01:20.176
Ollama wraps local models in a simple tool and API for desktops and developer workflows, with structured outputs and OpenAI-compatible endpoints.

12
00:01:20.456 --> 00:01:29.672
It is excellent for development and prototypes; for a shared, high-traffic service, a dedicated engine gives you more control over batching and memory.

13
00:01:29.952 --> 00:01:35.776
Text Generation Inference (TGI), Hugging Face’s own server, is now in maintenance mode.

14
00:01:36.056 --> 00:01:42.861
Its documentation says it will accept only minor fixes and recommends vLLM, SGLang, llama.

15
00:01:43.141 --> 00:01:45.659
cpp, or MLX going forward.

16
00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:51.229
You will still find TGI in older tutorials and deployments; don’t start new work on it.

17
00:01:51.509 --> 00:02:05.632
Hugging Face Inference Endpoints is a managed option: you pick a model and a GPU instance, and the service runs a supported engine (vLLM among them) with autoscaling, billing per minute while the endpoint is running.

18
00:02:05.912 --> 00:02:08.984
Cloud providers offer comparable managed services.

19
00:02:09.264 --> 00:02:16.091
Managed hosting removes the operations work in exchange for a markup on GPU time and less control.

20
00:02:16.371 --> 00:02:21.064
A reasonable default for the project: develop locally with Ollama or llama.

21
00:02:21.344 --> 00:02:30.069
cpp, benchmark and serve with vLLM on a single rented GPU, and say in your write-up what you would change for production traffic.
